    <main id="main-content">

      <!-- SLIDE 1 -->
      <article class="slide-article active" id="slide-1" aria-labelledby="slide1-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 1 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide1-heading">Executive Overview: ASC 740 &amp; Tax Accounting</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Accounting for income taxes bridges two distinct regulatory regimes: <strong>Financial Reporting (U.S. GAAP)</strong> and the <strong>Internal Revenue Code (IRC)</strong>.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Core Objective:</strong> Ensure that current and deferred tax consequences of all events recognized in financial statements are properly measured using the accrual method, reflecting future tax liabilities and future tax benefits.
        </div>
        <h3>Key Takeaways for This Dashboard</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Accrual Alignment:</strong> Income tax expense reported on the income statement reflects current period activities, regardless of when tax cash payments are due.</li>
          <li><strong>Balance Sheet Approach:</strong> Deferred taxes are determined by measuring temporary differences between the book values and tax bases of assets and liabilities.</li>
          <li><strong>Four-Step Method:</strong> Income tax expense serves as the balancing &ldquo;plug&rdquo; combining taxes payable and net changes in deferred tax accounts.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 2 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-2" aria-labelledby="slide2-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 2 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide2-heading">Conceptual Underpinnings: GAAP vs. Tax Objectives</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>The revenues, expenses, gains, and losses on a tax return frequently differ from those on the income statement because their foundational objectives diverge:</p>
        <div class="table-container">
          <table>
            <caption>Comparison of Financial Accounting and Tax Regulatory Objectives</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Dimension</th>
                <th scope="col">Financial Reporting (GAAP / ASC 740)</th>
                <th scope="col">Tax Authorities (IRS / IRC)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Governing Body</th>
                <td>Financial Accounting Standards Board (FASB)</td>
                <td>U.S. Congress &amp; Department of the Treasury</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Primary Objective</th>
                <td>Provide decision-useful financial information to investors, creditors, and capital markets</td>
                <td>Raise government revenue in an orderly manner and influence economic behavior</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Incentive Mechanisms</th>
                <td>Matches expenses against revenues systematically across operating periods</td>
                <td>Encourages capital investment (e.g., accelerated bonus depreciation) and discourages illegal acts (fines)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Measurement Basis</th>
                <td>Accrual basis accounting</td>
                <td>Modified cash basis and statutory cost-recovery rules</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 3 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-3" aria-labelledby="slide3-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 3 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide3-heading">The Four-Step Measurement Sequence</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Under ASC 740, income tax expense is never calculated directly by multiplying pretax accounting income by the tax rate. Instead, it is derived using a standardized four-step sequence:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Step 1: Calculate Tax Currently Payable</strong><br>
            Multiply taxable income from the current tax return by the current enacted tax rate:
            <br><em>Tax Payable = Taxable Income &times; Current Enacted Tax Rate</em>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Step 2: Calculate Ending Balances for DTAs and DTLs</strong><br>
            Determine cumulative future taxable and deductible amounts based on year-end book-tax basis differences, multiplied by the scheduled enacted tax rates for future reversal years.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Step 3: Calculate Period Changes in Deferred Accounts</strong><br>
            Compute the net debit or credit needed to adjust each deferred tax asset and liability from its beginning balance to the required ending balance from Step 2.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Step 4: Solve for Income Tax Expense as the Plug</strong><br>
            Record the tax journal entry combining tax payable (Step 1) and deferred account adjustments (Step 3). The balancing amount is total <strong>Income Tax Expense</strong>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 4 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-4" aria-labelledby="slide4-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 4 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide4-heading">Taxonomy of Temporary Differences (2&times;2 Matrix)</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Temporary differences arise when revenues or expenses are recognized in different years for book versus tax purposes. They originate in one period and completely reverse over time:</p>
        <div class="table-container">
          <table>
            <caption>Classification of Temporary Differences (Illustration 16-1 Structure)</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Timing Pattern</th>
                <th scope="col">Revenues / Gains</th>
                <th scope="col">Expenses / Losses</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Reported on Income Statement First, Tax Return Later</th>
                <td>
                  <strong>Creates Future Taxable Amounts (DTL)</strong><br>
                  <em>Examples:</em> Installment sales of property, unrealized holding gains on fair-value investments.
                </td>
                <td>
                  <strong>Creates Future Deductible Amounts (DTA)</strong><br>
                  <em>Examples:</em> Estimated product warranty expense, accrued loss contingencies, postretirement benefit costs.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Reported on Tax Return First, Income Statement Later</th>
                <td>
                  <strong>Creates Future Deductible Amounts (DTA)</strong><br>
                  <em>Examples:</em> Subscriptions collected in advance, unearned rental receipts, customer deposits.
                </td>
                <td>
                  <strong>Creates Future Taxable Amounts (DTL)</strong><br>
                  <em>Examples:</em> Accelerated tax depreciation (MACRS) in excess of straight-line book depreciation, prepaid insurance deductible upon payment.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 5 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-5" aria-labelledby="slide5-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 5 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide5-heading">Deferred Tax Liabilities (DTL) &amp; Future Taxable Amounts</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>A <strong>Deferred Tax Liability (DTL)</strong> represents taxes that have been deferred to future periods because current tax deductions or delayed tax revenues cause taxable income to be lower than book income today.</p>
        <h3>Primary Examples</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Accelerated Depreciation (MACRS):</strong> Tax laws allow full or accelerated cost recovery in early years. In later years, tax depreciation falls below straight-line book depreciation, creating future taxable amounts.</li>
          <li><strong>Installment Sales:</strong> Revenue is recognized immediately upon sale under GAAP, but taxed only as cash is collected in subsequent years.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Balance Sheet Criterion:</strong> A DTL is present whenever an <em>Asset&rsquo;s Book Value exceeds its Tax Basis</em>, or a <em>Liability&rsquo;s Book Value is less than its Tax Basis</em>.
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 6 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-6" aria-labelledby="slide6-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 6 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide6-heading">Deferred Tax Assets (DTA) &amp; Future Deductible Amounts</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>A <strong>Deferred Tax Asset (DTA)</strong> represents future tax savings resulting from having paid taxes in advance or having recognized expenses for financial accounting that cannot yet be deducted on the tax return.</p>
        <h3>Primary Examples</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Estimated Product Warranties:</strong> Accrued as expense under GAAP when goods are sold; deductible for tax purposes only when cash is paid to satisfy claims in future years.</li>
          <li><strong>Unearned Subscription/Rent Revenue:</strong> Taxed immediately upon receipt under cash-basis tax rules; recognized as revenue in later years as obligations are fulfilled.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Balance Sheet Criterion:</strong> A DTA is present whenever a <em>Liability&rsquo;s Book Value exceeds its Tax Basis</em>, or an <em>Asset&rsquo;s Book Value is less than its Tax Basis</em>.
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 7 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-7" aria-labelledby="slide7-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 7 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide7-heading">Valuation Allowance Assessment for DTAs</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Deferred tax assets are recognized in full for all future deductible differences, but they are subsequently reduced by a <strong>contra-asset valuation allowance</strong> if realization is uncertain.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>The Standard (ASC 740-10-30-5):</strong> A valuation allowance is established if, based on the weight of available evidence, it is <strong>&ldquo;more likely than not&rdquo;</strong> (a likelihood greater than 50%) that some or all of the deferred tax asset will not be realized.
        </div>
        <h3>Evaluating Evidentiary Factors</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Negative Evidence:</strong> Cumulative historical operating losses, anticipated future industry downturns, or expiring carryforward windows.</li>
          <li><strong>Positive Evidence:</strong> Existing contracts, backlog of orders, profitable historical track record, and prudent tax-planning strategies.</li>
          <li><strong>Journal Entry:</strong> Debit <em>Income Tax Expense</em>, Credit <em>Valuation Allowance</em> (reduces reported net income).</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 8 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-8" aria-labelledby="slide8-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 8 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide8-heading">Permanent Differences &amp; Effective Tax Rate (ETR)</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Permanent differences</strong> arise from statutory tax rules where transactions never affect taxable income or taxes payable. Because they never reverse, they produce <strong>no deferred tax consequences</strong>.</p>
        <h3>Common Examples</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Interest earned on state and municipal government bonds (tax-free).</li>
          <li>Fines and statutory penalties resulting from law violations (nondeductible).</li>
          <li>Life insurance proceeds received upon executive death (nondeductible premiums / tax-free proceeds).</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Effective Tax Rate Impact:</strong> 
          <br><em>Effective Tax Rate = Total Income Tax Expense &divide; Pretax Accounting Income</em>
          <br>Tax-exempt income pulls the effective rate <em>below</em> the statutory rate; nondeductible expenses push the effective rate <em>above</em> the statutory rate.
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 9 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-9" aria-labelledby="slide9-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 9 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide9-heading">Enacted Tax Rates &amp; Remeasurement Rules</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Deferred tax assets and liabilities must be measured using the <strong>currently enacted tax rate</strong> scheduled to apply in the future years when temporary differences are expected to reverse.</p>
        <h3>Key Regulatory Principles</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Reliability over Relevance:</strong> Anticipated tax legislation is never recognized until signed into law by the executive branch.</li>
          <li><strong>Immediate Period-of-Enactment Adjustment:</strong> When a new tax rate is enacted, all existing DTAs and DTLs are remeasured immediately. The entire adjustment is recognized in <em>Income Tax Expense from Continuing Operations</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Asymmetrical Impact of Rate Cuts:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Net DTL Firms (e.g., GladCo):</strong> Lower rates reduce future liabilities, generating a credit to tax expense (tax benefit) that boosts current net income.</li>
              <li><strong>Net DTA Firms (e.g., SadCo):</strong> Lower rates reduce the value of future tax deductions, forcing a debit write-down to tax expense that reduces current net income.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <!-- SLIDE 10 -->
      <article class="slide-article" id="slide-10" aria-labelledby="slide10-heading">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <div>
            <span class="slide-badge">Slide 10 of 10</span>
            <h2 id="slide10-heading">Financial Statement Presentation &amp; Disclosures</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Presentation standards under U.S. GAAP (ASU 2015-17) require streamlined, noncurrent balance sheet classification with comprehensive footnote reconciliations:</p>
        <h3>Balance Sheet Presentation Guidelines</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>All Noncurrent:</strong> All deferred tax liabilities, deferred tax assets, and valuation allowances are classified as <strong>noncurrent</strong> on the balance sheet.</li>
          <li><strong>Jurisdictional Netting:</strong> DTAs and DTLs within the same tax-paying component and tax jurisdiction must be netted into a single noncurrent amount. Accounts across separate tax jurisdictions cannot be netted.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Mandatory Disclosure Notes</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Breakdown of total tax expense into current provision and deferred provision.</li>
          <li>Comprehensive inventory of components comprising deferred tax assets and liabilities.</li>
          <li>Reconciliation of beginning and ending valuation allowance balances.</li>
          <li>Numerical reconciliation between the statutory federal tax rate and the reported effective tax rate.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

    </main>

    <footer>
      <p>Accessible Educational Companion &middot; Grounded in FASB ASC Topic 740 &middot; WCAG 2.1 Level AA Compliant</p>
    </footer>
  </div>

  <script>
    let currentSlide = 1;
    const totalSlides = 10;
    let isFullTranscriptView = false;

    const slideTitles = [
      "Executive Overview",
      "Conceptual Underpinnings",
      "The 4-Step Process",
      "Temporary Differences Taxonomy",
      "Deferred Tax Liabilities (DTL)",
      "Deferred Tax Assets (DTA)",
      "Valuation Allowance Assessment",
      "Permanent Differences & ETR",
      "Enacted Tax Rates & Remeasurement",
      "Financial Statement Presentation"
    ];

    function updateSlideDisplay() {
      if (isFullTranscriptView) return;

      for (let i = 1; i <= totalSlides; i++) {
        const slide = document.getElementById(`slide-${i}`);
        if (i === currentSlide) {
          slide.classList.add("active");
        } else {
          slide.classList.remove("active");
        }
      }

      document.getElementById("prevBtn").disabled = currentSlide === 1;
      document.getElementById("nextBtn").disabled = currentSlide === totalSlides;
      document.getElementById("slideSelect").value = currentSlide;

      // Announce slide change to screen readers
      const announcer = document.getElementById("srAnnounce");
      announcer.textContent = `Viewing Slide ${currentSlide} of ${totalSlides}: ${slideTitles[currentSlide - 1]}`;
    }

    function navigateSlide(direction) {
      if (isFullTranscriptView) {
        toggleAllSlides();
      }
      currentSlide = Math.min(Math.max(currentSlide + direction, 1), totalSlides);
      updateSlideDisplay();
      document.getElementById(`slide-${currentSlide}`).focus();
    }

    function jumpToSlide(slideNum) {
      if (isFullTranscriptView) {
        toggleAllSlides();
      }
      currentSlide = slideNum;
      updateSlideDisplay();
    }

    function toggleAllSlides() {
      const toggleBtn = document.getElementById("toggleViewBtn");
      isFullTranscriptView = !isFullTranscriptView;

      for (let i = 1; i <= totalSlides; i++) {
        const slide = document.getElementById(`slide-${i}`);
        if (isFullTranscriptView) {
          slide.classList.add("active");
        } else {
          if (i === currentSlide) {
            slide.classList.add("active");
          } else {
            slide.classList.remove("active");
          }
        }
      }

      if (isFullTranscriptView) {
        toggleBtn.textContent = "Switch to Single-Slide View";
        document.getElementById("prevBtn").disabled = true;
        document.getElementById("nextBtn").disabled = true;
        document.getElementById("srAnnounce").textContent = "Viewing full transcript of all 10 slides.";
      } else {
        toggleBtn.textContent = "View All Slides as Transcript";
        updateSlideDisplay();
      }
    }

    // Initialize display state
    updateSlideDisplay();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
